{:record/kind :evidence, :record/id "e-codexpilot-specialize-the-library-Gaussian-Fourier-transform-before-building-a-contour-proof", :record/payload {:evidence/body {:hook "Before constructing a classical contour proof, search for the theorem under the library's transform normalization and treat the task as an adapter when the mathematical result is already present.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :why "Evaluate the Fourier transform of a Gaussian under a different sign, coercion, and normalization convention.", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "specialize-the-library-Gaussian-Fourier-transform-before-building-a-contour-proof", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "Before constructing a classical contour proof, search for the theorem under the library's transform normalization and treat the task as an adapter when the mathematical result is already present.", :body {:hook "For `∫ exp(i y x - x²)`, instantiate `fourierIntegral_gaussian` first; the remaining work is exponent normalization and the algebraic identity `I²=-1`, not a new contour argument.", :capture-kind :runner-library-theorem-specialization-and-ground-control-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Evaluate the Fourier transform of a Gaussian under a different sign, coercion, and normalization convention.", :key-api ["fourierIntegral_gaussian" "Complex.I_sq"], :witness {:axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :declarations ["gaussian_fourier_transform" "complete_square"]}, :rule "Before constructing a classical contour proof, search for the theorem under the library's transform normalization and treat the task as an adapter when the mathematical result is already present.", :boundary "This route evaluates the Gaussian transform only. It does not justify the nonintegrable Fresnel set integral, whose classical value requires an improper-limit statement.", :how-to-apply ["Search the installed Gaussian/Fourier namespace before implementing the documented contour-shift plan." "Instantiate `fourierIntegral_gaussian` with the real Gaussian coefficient specialized to one and the frequency coerced to `ℂ`." "Rewrite the integrand into Mathlib's product and exponent conventions." "Normalize the returned square-root and exponential factors." "For the companion completion-of-the-square identity, expand polynomially and rewrite with `Complex.I_sq`."], :route-override {:existing-memory-id "e-codexpilot-override-a-documented-proof-route-when-component-evidence-favors-another", :use-status :used, :documented-route "Build a contour proof.", :implemented-route "Specialize the existing Gaussian Fourier theorem and normalize its statement.", :paired-discipline "The same evidence-based override rejected the separate Fresnel target once its Bochner semantics were machine-refuted."}}, :promotion {:reviewed-by "claude-9", :verdict :approve, :warrant "operator-delegated: in-use evaluation (proof-in-pudding; owner draft review claude-6 2026-07-28; Joe's standing direction)"}}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree-lane", :evidence/tags [:memory :mathematics :codex-pilot :solve-lane], :evidence/at "2026-07-30T23:10:03.804124508Z", :evidence/type :memory, :evidence/subject {:ref/type :mission, :ref/id "M-codex-sorry-loop"}, :evidence/author "codex-5", :evidence/claim-type :assert, :evidence/id "e-codexpilot-specialize-the-library-Gaussian-Fourier-transform-before-building-a-contour-proof"}}
{:record/kind :evidence, :record/id "e-codexpilot-split-compact-support-Laplace-entireness-on-integrability", :record/payload {:evidence/body {:hook "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :why "Prove an integral-defined transform is entire from compact support when the statement does not separately assume measurability or integrability.", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "split-compact-support-Laplace-entireness-on-integrability", :runner-model :codex, :kind :feedback, :how-to-apply "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :body {:hook "Compact support without measurability does not force Bochner integrability: prove the integrable branch by bounded-support domination, and show the nonintegrable branch makes every defining integral zero.", :capture-kind :runner-integrability-split-and-ground-control-axiom-verification, :problem-class "Prove an integral-defined transform is entire from compact support when the statement does not separately assume measurability or integrability.", :key-api ["IsCompact.bddAbove" "hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le" "integral_undef" "analyticOn_const"], :witness {:declaration "laplace_transform_entire_of_compact_support", :axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"], :sorryax false, :statement "`compactSupportOnHalfLine f → AnalyticOn ℂ (laplaceTransform f) Set.univ`"}, :semantic-split {:integrable-branch "Compact support supplies bounded derivative dominators; ordinary parametric integration carries the proof.", :nonintegrable-branch "Mathlib's totalized Bochner integral makes every nonintegrable defining integral zero, so the transform is identically zero and hence analytic."}, :rule "When an integral transform theorem omits measurability, audit the totalized integral semantics explicitly: a proof may need an integrable/nonintegrable split rather than an illicit inferred hypothesis.", :boundary "The nonintegrable branch proves the formal statement through Mathlib's zero convention; it does not claim the classical transform of a nonmeasurable function has analytic content.", :how-to-apply ["Split on `Integrable f` for the positive-half-line measure instead of silently assuming compact support supplies measurability." "In the integrable branch, bound `t`, the exponential factor, and the derivative factor uniformly on the compact support." "Use local dominated differentiation at each complex parameter to obtain entire analyticity." "In the nonintegrable branch, prove multiplication by the nonvanishing exponential kernel cannot make the product integrable." "Apply `integral_undef` at every parameter, rewrite the Laplace transform as the zero function, and use analyticity of constants."]}, :promotion {:reviewed-by "claude-9", :verdict :approve, :warrant "operator-delegated: in-use evaluation (proof-in-pudding; owner draft review claude-6 2026-07-28; Joe's standing direction)"}}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree-lane", :evidence/tags [:memory :mathematics :codex-pilot :solve-lane], :evidence/at "2026-07-30T23:09:59.842593760Z", :evidence/type :memory, :evidence/subject {:ref/type :mission, :ref/id "M-codex-sorry-loop"}, :evidence/author "codex-5", :evidence/claim-type :assert, :evidence/id "e-codexpilot-split-compact-support-Laplace-entireness-on-integrability"}}
{:record/kind :evidence, :record/id "e-codexpilot-spread-small-pairwise-correlations-across-growing-triangular-support", :record/payload {:evidence/body {:hook "To test a uniform-constant claim derived from pairwise bounds, build a triangular family whose support grows with the index; compare the pairwise scale with the square-summed aggregate scale before attempting the proof.", :scribe-author "codex-5", :draft-source "holes/labs/M-codex-sorry-loop/", :name "spread-small-pairwise-correlations-across-growing-triangular-support", :runner-model :codex, :kind :reference, :how-to-apply "To test a uniform-constant claim derived from pairwise bounds, build a triangular family whose support grows with the index; compare the pairwise scale with the square-summed aggregate scale before attempting the proof.", :body {:boundary "The construction proves that any valid conclusion must permit at least the displayed `sqrt(m)` loss or impose stronger aggregate correlation control. It does not prove that a particular `C*sqrt(m)*2^(-m)` upper bound is sufficient.", :capture-kind :runner-counterexample-and-ground-control-checked-asymptotics, :rule "To test a uniform-constant claim derived from pairwise bounds, build a triangular family whose support grows with the index; compare the pairwise scale with the square-summed aggregate scale before attempting the proof.", :target-class "Refute a uniform-constant approximation estimate inferred only from small pairwise correlations.", :construction {:ambient-space "`ℓ²(ℕ)` with coordinate basis `e_i`", :coefficients ["`a_m = 2^(-(m+1))`" "`b_m = sqrt(1 - m * a_m^2)`"], :vectors "`f_m = a_m * ∑_{i<m} e_i + b_m * e_m`"}, :checks [{:property :unit-norm, :calculation "`‖f_m‖² = m*a_m² + b_m² = 1`"} {:property :pairwise-hypothesis, :calculation "For `n < m`, `|⟪f_n,f_m⟫| = a_m * (n*a_n + b_n) ≤ (3/2)*a_m ≤ 2^(-m)`."} {:property :gram-schmidt-output, :calculation "The family is triangular with positive diagonal, so normalized Gram–Schmidt returns 